198
00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:30.080
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): key pre-rotation. Keys and digital identity come in pairs, a private key and a public key. You keep the private key secret, usually stored in a secure digital wallet. The public key, on the other hand, is meant to be shared, for example, by publishing it in a DID document so that others can use it to verify you.

199
00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:46.369
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With these keys, you can do powerful things. You can authenticate yourself online and log into services without passwords. You can also digitally sign documents, such as university… such as a university signing degrees for its graduates, and anyone can check that signature against the public key.

200
00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:57.710
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): But there's a catch. If your private key is ever compromised, maybe your wallet gets hacked or your storage system is tricked, your identity could be at risk, meaning that someone could impersonate you online.

201
00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:10.790
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That's why it's good practice to rotate your keys, retire the old ones, and start using the new one. In DID systems, these updates may be recorded in the DID document data, so that anyone resolving the DID knows which key is current.

202
00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:24.619
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Still, that raises a problem. What if an attacker steals your key and performs the rotation before you can? Suddenly, they look like the rightful controller of your did, and you can't reclaim control because the system now trusts their key.

203
00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.849
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This is where key pre-rotation comes in.

204
00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:41.120
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With pre-rotation, every time you create a key, you also generate the next one in advance. Instead of publishing the full future key, you publish a cryptographic digest, a commitment to the key you'll use next.

205
00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:48.189
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Later, when you rotate, you reveal the actual key, and anyone can check that it matches the digest you committed earlier.

206
00:25:48.220 --> 00:26:02.570
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Where this pre-rotation data is stored is specific to each DID method, and is not part of the DID core specification. Some DID methods, like DIDWebS and DIDWebVH, support key rotation pre-rotation, and other methods do not.

207
00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:18.619
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With key pre-rotation, even if someone compromises your current private key, they can't hijack your DID, because they don't know the next key you've already committed to. Where supported, pre-rotation makes key rotation predictable and tamper-resistant, securing DIDs against takeover.

